  "debates": [
   "How the sign later in the chapter should be read: Ellicott sets out one view that keeps it within the prophet&rsquo;s own horizon and another that takes the evangelist&rsquo;s citation as settling the whole meaning, lists the difficulties of the second at length, and proposes a third way in which the facts being given, the prophecy was shown to have a meaning that need never have been present to the prophet&rsquo;s mind.",
   "What the verb rendered is confederate with means: Keil and Delitzsch take it as settled down upon, denoting elsewhere a hostile invasion but here one army arriving to support another, while Calvin reports three different derivations, one giving that the one king leans on the other as on a friend&rsquo;s arm."
  ],
  "outliers": [
   "Ellicott says the theory that the mother of the child was one of the king&rsquo;s household and that she bore Hezekiah breaks down under the test of dates, Hezekiah being already a boy of nine.",
   "Ellicott turns the absence of any later Jewish tradition about a virgin birth into an argument for the historical character of the nativity narratives, since they ran contrary to prevailing expectation.",
   "Calvin insists this is the second war and not the first, and that the two kings came again because they judged Ahaz worn out, which is what makes the deliverance a miracle.",
   "Henry supposes many useful sermons were preached under the previous reign and never published, and that under a wicked king the prophet may have had less opportunity at court and so wrote the more."
  ],
  "themes": [
   "A conspiracy against Jerusalem",
   "A court shaken like trees in wind",
   "The question the chapter will raise"
  ],
